WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.308
Idea. Blood has four components. Three of them float in a liquid: red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets. The liquid is pale yellow, and it is called plasma.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:23.833
To identify each one, look at three clues: its size, its shape, and whether it has a nucleus. The nucleus is the part of a cell that holds its DNA. It looks like a dark shape.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:39.772
Method. Red blood cells are the small round ones, and there are lots of them. Each is a biconcave disc: thinner in the middle, so the centre looks pale. They have no nucleus. They are full of haemoglobin, which carries oxygen.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:56.311
White blood cells are bigger, and they have a nucleus. A phagocyte has a nucleus with lobes, like bumps. It engulfs pathogens. Engulf means it takes them in and digests them. A pathogen is a microorganism that causes disease.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:09.688
A lymphocyte is the other white cell. Its nucleus is large and round, and it fills most of the cell. Lymphocytes make antibodies: proteins that stick to pathogens and help to destroy them.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:18.771
Platelets are the tiny bits. They are fragments of cells, not whole cells, and they have no nucleus. They help the blood to clot at a cut.

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:35.711
Plasma is the liquid between the cells. It is mostly water. It carries dissolved glucose, carbon dioxide, urea, hormones and clotting proteins round the body. Urea is a waste made in the liver. Hormones are chemical messengers.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:48.539
For a tick table, work one row at a time. Has a nucleus? Only the white cells. Contains haemoglobin? Only the red cells. A whole cell? Red and white cells, but not platelets.

00:01:49.650 --> 00:02:01.756
Worked example. Now a real exam question. The diagram shows a sample of human blood, with labels X, Y and Z. Name components X, Y and Z. Three marks.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:15.208
X points to the empty space between the cells. Nothing is drawn there, so it is the liquid: plasma. Y points to a small cell with a pale dip in the middle and no nucleus: a red blood cell.

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:28.691
Z points to a larger cell with a dark, lobed nucleus. A lobed nucleus means a phagocyte, a kind of white blood cell. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:37.726
Common mistake. A common mistake is to call platelets cells. Platelets are fragments of cells. They have no nucleus.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:46.032
Another mistake is to give one white cell both jobs. Phagocytes engulf pathogens. Lymphocytes make antibodies.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:59.500
Your turn. A patient's blood has very few platelets. Suggest what problem this causes. Then name the white cell with a large round nucleus, and give its job. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:07.478
Answer. Part a: platelets help the blood to clot. With very few, a cut keeps bleeding for a long time.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:14.072
Part b: the white cell with a large round nucleus is a lymphocyte. It makes antibodies.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:21.425
So, every time: look at size, shape and nucleus. Then use the exact name, and give its job.
